From 3aa8943c20d366ccc0a64a6ada363fb318d04d70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bsp2020 <bsp2020@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:32:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] moe cache: GPU-safe pageable master for --host-experts
 mmap through a sparse registration

With --host-experts mmap the expert master is the pageable model mapping, which a GPU without XNACK cannot read (issue
cache, no extra RAM) and registers (madvise + mlock + hipHostRegister ReadOnly) exactly the expert ranges a GPU read
touches: the experts not resident in the arena.  Every cold read takes its id from the eager remap, which registers
the expert first; every arena fill, seed fill and routed fill registers its source expert and copies it with a kernel
from the alias.  Runs no GPU read can reach are unregistered by a trim that synchronizes every device first: at
sizing, and every 10 s while the registration is over GGML_MOE_SREG_BUDGET_MIB (default 40 % of RAM).

At sizing a table reads its master in parallel, fills its empty arena with provisional experts through a pinned
staging buffer and registers its cold set.  A prefill reads the arena + the registered alias too: a small prefill
ubatch gets the routed experts (and the tail-pad neighbour) copied to their offsets in the op's input, a staged one
gets the whole table assembled on the copy stream (moe_cache_stage_table, ordered after the compute stream as the
pinned staging is); the pinned staging declines a non-pinned master.  The warm-up pass (every expert) is declined and
streamed by the scheduler from the page cache.  Under -sm layer a routed op stays on the device that owns its tables.
A master that cannot be aliased or registered is declined by the cache (the scheduler's copy serves it), never read
pageable.  Pinned and pool masters are unchanged.  POSIX only: on Windows no alias is made.
GGML_MOE_CACHE_SPARSE_REG=0: decline every pageable master.
